    prerequisites: ["Normalization", "Joins", "Transactions", "Concurrency", "Indexing"],
    concept: "Same rehearsal idea as the Operating Systems subject's interview-questions lesson: you already know every individual DBMS concept - now practice retrieving and connecting them under interview-style pressure, in random order, often as rapid follow-ups.\n\nA strong DBMS answer follows the same three-part shape as any solid technical answer: precise definition, a concrete example, and the key trade-off. Asked 'what is normalization', a strong answer is: \"Normalization is splitting a table into smaller, well-structured tables to eliminate redundancy and the update/insertion/deletion anomalies that redundancy causes - for example, storing a teacher's office number once in a Teachers table instead of repeating it on every enrollment row. The trade-off is that a fully normalized schema often needs more JOINs to answer common questions, which some systems deliberately avoid by denormalizing specific read-heavy tables.\"\n\nThe five topics worth over-preparing, because they're asked almost everywhere regardless of company or role: normalization (with a live example you can walk through), the difference between joins (INNER vs LEFT especially), ACID properties (each with its own example), the concurrency anomalies (dirty read, non-repeatable read, phantom read, lost update), and indexing trade-offs (why it speeds reads but costs writes).",
    keyPoints: ["Structure DBMS answers as: precise definition -> concrete example -> the key trade-off, exactly like the OS subject's interview-answer structure", "The five highest-frequency topics: normalization (with a live example), INNER vs LEFT join, ACID properties, concurrency anomalies, and indexing trade-offs", "Interviewers frequently chain follow-ups across topics (e.g. normalization -> how does that affect join performance) - practice connecting ideas, not recalling them in isolation"],
    commonMistakes: ["Reciting the ACID acronym without being able to give a distinct concrete example for each individual letter - interviewers often ask for one specifically, not all four generically", "Not being able to actually WRITE a working SQL query live (a JOIN, a GROUP BY with HAVING) when asked - conceptual knowledge alone isn't enough for most DBMS rounds"],
    interviewTips: ["Practice writing SQL by hand (not just reading it) - actually typing/writing a JOIN or GROUP BY query from a blank page reveals gaps that just reading solved examples doesn't", "Be ready to walk through normalizing a genuinely messy example table live, step by step through 1NF/2NF/3NF - this is one of THE most common DBMS interview exercises across companies"],
    realWorldApplications: ["The same 'define, example, trade-off' structure from the OS subject's interview-prep lesson applies directly here too - it's a reusable interview skill across every technical subject, not unique to DBMS"],
