    goingDeeper: "Beyond single-concept questions, strong candidates get asked design-style questions that combine several DBMS ideas at once - e.g. 'design a database schema for an online bookstore, including how you'd handle concurrent purchases of the last copy of a book' (answer needs ER modeling, normalization, AND a transaction/isolation-level strategy together). Practicing these combined-design questions, not just isolated definitions, is what separates a strong DBMS round from a merely adequate one.",
