    whatYoullLearn: ["What a class actually is (a blueprint, not a thing itself)", "The difference between a class and an object", "How to define a simple class with fields and methods"],
    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "A cookie cutter is not a cookie - you can't eat it, and there's only ever one cutter even if you stamp out fifty cookies from it. But every cookie it stamps out shares the exact same shape, because they all came from the same cutter. A class is exactly that cookie cutter: a blueprint defining what SHAPE something will have (what data it holds, what actions it can perform) - but the class itself is never the actual 'thing' you use; it's the template.\n\nTechnically, a class defines fields (the data an object of this class will hold - like a Cookie's flavor and size) and methods (the actions an object can perform - like bake() or decorate()). Every object created 'from' a class (covered in the next lesson) gets its own independent copy of the fields defined by the class, while sharing the same method definitions.\n\nThis separation - blueprint vs. actual thing - is the single most foundational idea in all of Object-Oriented Programming, and every other OOP concept in this subject (inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation) is really just a different way of extending or restricting what a class blueprint can do.",
    keyPoints: ["A class is a blueprint/template - it defines structure (fields) and behavior (methods), but is not itself a usable 'thing'", "Fields are the data a class defines; methods are the actions it defines", "Every object made from a class gets its own copy of the fields, while sharing the same method definitions"],
    commonMistakes: ["Treating 'class' and 'object' as interchangeable words - a class is the blueprint; an object is one specific thing built from it (covered fully next lesson)", "Forgetting that fields and methods are two DIFFERENT kinds of class members - fields hold data, methods define behavior"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to define 'class' precisely in one sentence without using the word 'object' in the definition itself - this trips up a surprising number of candidates", "Know the cookie-cutter (or blueprint) analogy well enough to explain it fluently - interviewers like concrete analogies over textbook wording"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Every app you've used is built from dozens or hundreds of classes - a `User` class, a `Product` class, an `Order` class - each defining the shape of one kind of 'thing' the app deals with"],
