    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "A placement aptitude test isn't really testing whether you CAN do percentages or ratios - almost everyone technically can, given enough time. It's testing whether you can do them in under a minute, under pressure, alongside 30 other questions. That reframing matters: the actual skill being tested is PATTERN RECOGNITION under a clock, not raw mathematical ability.\n\nThe recurring topic list across nearly every service-company drive (TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Cognizant, Accenture) is genuinely narrow and predictable: percentages (and percentage CHANGE specifically - a 20% increase then a 20% decrease does NOT return to the original value, a classic trap), ratios and proportions, profit/loss/discount, simple and compound interest, and time-speed-distance (including the 'two trains'/'boats and streams' family of problems, which are really all the same underlying relative-speed idea wearing different costumes).\n\nThe single highest-leverage study habit here: for every problem type, don't just solve it once - solve enough variations that you recognize the TYPE within the first two lines of the question, and immediately recall the shortcut formula rather than re-deriving it from first principles every single time. A student who's seen 20 profit-and-loss variations recognizes the 21st in five seconds; a student solving it fresh from first principles every time will run out of clock before finishing the section.",
    keyPoints: ["The aptitude round tests speed and pattern recognition under time pressure, not raw mathematical capability - nearly everyone CAN do the math given unlimited time", "Percentage change is NOT symmetric: a 20% increase followed by a 20% decrease does not return to the original value - a classic, frequently tested trap", "The recurring topic list is narrow and predictable: percentages, ratios, profit/loss, interest, time-speed-distance - practice enough variations to recognize the TYPE instantly, not re-derive from scratch"],
    commonMistakes: ["Assuming a percentage increase and an equal percentage decrease cancel out - they don't, since the second percentage is calculated on a different (already-changed) base value", "Re-deriving a formula from first principles every time instead of recognizing the question type and recalling the shortcut, burning far too much time per question"],
    interviewTips: ["Time yourself explicitly on practice sets - a huge share of aptitude-round failure is a genuinely correct approach that simply ran out of time, not incorrect math", "Keep a small personal 'formula sheet' of shortcuts for each recurring type (percentage change, compound interest, relative speed) and drill it until recall is instant"],
