    goingDeeper: "Calendar and clock-angle puzzles (both classic 'problem solving' sub-types) each rely on one small, memorizable set of underlying facts - the calendar puzzle family reduces to knowing how many odd days a given number of years contributes, and clock-angle problems reduce to knowing the hour and minute hands each move at a fixed, calculable degrees-per-minute rate - treating these as 'memorize the one underlying fact, then apply it' rather than 'solve fresh from scratch' is the same recognize-the-type efficiency principle from the Basic Mathematics lesson, applied here.",
