    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "A compiler translates source code written in one language (like C or Java) into another form - typically machine code or bytecode - that a computer (or a virtual machine) can actually execute. This translation happens in distinct PHASES, each handing its output to the next: lexical analysis, parsing (syntax analysis), semantic analysis, intermediate code generation, optimization, and code generation - this subject's two modules (Front End, Back End) cover roughly the first half and second half of this pipeline respectively.\n\nLEXICAL ANALYSIS (performed by a component called the LEXER or scanner) is the very FIRST phase: it reads the raw source code, character by character, and groups those characters into TOKENS - the smallest meaningful units, like keywords (`if`, `while`), identifiers (`total`, `x`), operators (`+`, `==`), and literals (`42`, `\"hello\"`). Given `total = price + 5;`, a lexer produces something like: IDENTIFIER(total), ASSIGN(=), IDENTIFIER(price), PLUS(+), NUMBER(5), SEMICOLON(;) - the lexer discards irrelevant details like whitespace and comments entirely, since they carry no meaning for later phases.\n\nUnder the hood, a lexer is built from REGULAR EXPRESSIONS (defining the pattern each token type matches, like 'one or more digits' for a NUMBER token) compiled down into a FINITE AUTOMATON (a state machine that reads one character at a time, transitioning between states, until it recognizes a complete token) - this is precisely why understanding regular expressions (useful directly in text processing, covered in this platform's Bash path) gives you real insight into how a lexer's pattern-matching actually works internally.",
