    prerequisites: ["Lexical Analysis"],
    concept: "PARSING (syntax analysis) takes the lexer's token stream and checks whether it forms a STRUCTURALLY VALID sentence in the language's grammar - genuinely analogous to checking whether a sentence follows English grammar rules, even before considering whether it actually MEANS anything sensible (that's semantic analysis's job, the next phase). `if (x > 5 {` fails parsing (a missing closing parenthesis breaks the grammar's structure), even though a human might guess what was intended.\n\nA language's valid syntax is formally defined by a CONTEXT-FREE GRAMMAR - a set of rules like `statement -> IF ( expression ) statement` - describing exactly which token sequences are structurally legal. The parser's job is to determine whether a given token stream can be DERIVED from the grammar's start symbol by repeatedly applying these rules - if it can, the parser succeeds and typically builds a PARSE TREE (or the more compact ABSTRACT SYNTAX TREE, AST) representing the code's structure; if it can't, a syntax error is reported.\n\nParsers come in two broad families: TOP-DOWN parsers start from the grammar's start symbol and work DOWNWARD, trying to match the input by predicting which rule applies next (recursive-descent parsers, genuinely common in hand-written parsers, work this way); BOTTOM-UP parsers start from the actual input tokens and work UPWARD, repeatedly recognizing and combining smaller pieces into larger grammatical structures until the whole input is reduced to the start symbol (LR parsers, common in parser-generator tools, work this way) - both approaches reach the same correctness goal through genuinely different strategies.",
    keyPoints: ["Parsing checks whether a token stream is STRUCTURALLY valid according to the language's grammar - like checking English grammar, before considering whether the sentence actually means anything", "A context-free grammar formally defines a language's valid syntax as a set of derivation rules; a successful parse produces a parse tree or abstract syntax tree (AST)", "Top-down parsers (like recursive descent) predict downward from the grammar's start symbol; bottom-up parsers (like LR) recognize and combine upward from the actual input tokens"],
