    whatYoullLearn: ["Why compilers generate an intermediate representation instead of jumping straight to machine code", "Three-address code as a common, concrete intermediate form", "How an intermediate representation enables retargeting a compiler to multiple CPU architectures"],
    prerequisites: ["Syntax Trees"],
    concept: "Rather than translating an AST DIRECTLY into a specific CPU's machine code, most real compilers generate an INTERMEDIATE REPRESENTATION (IR) first - a lower-level, simplified form that's still independent of any particular target machine. This is a genuinely deliberate architectural choice, not an unnecessary extra step: it lets a compiler's FRONT END (lexer, parser, semantic analysis - everything covered so far in this subject) stay completely separate from its BACK END (everything from here forward - optimization and code generation), meaning a NEW source language only needs a NEW front end (translating INTO the same IR), and a NEW target CPU architecture only needs a NEW back end (translating FROM the same IR) - avoiding an otherwise combinatorial explosion of separate compilers for every language-times-architecture pairing.\n\nTHREE-ADDRESS CODE is a common, concrete IR form: each instruction has AT MOST three operands (typically one result plus two operands), like `t1 = price + 5; total = t1;` - genuinely simple, uniform instructions that are dramatically easier to analyze and optimize systematically than an arbitrarily-nested AST expression tree would be, since every operation is already broken down into the smallest possible individual steps.\n\nThis IR-based design is precisely why the same compiler FRONT END (say, for C) can target wildly different architectures (x86, ARM, RISC-V) just by swapping in a different BACK END - and it's exactly the architecture behind real tools like LLVM, whose entire ecosystem is built around one shared, well-designed IR that many different language front ends (Clang for C/C++, Rust's compiler, Swift's compiler) all compile down into.",
    keyPoints: ["Compilers generate an Intermediate Representation (IR) between the front end (lexer/parser/semantic analysis) and back end (optimization/code generation), rather than jumping straight from AST to machine code", "This separation lets a new source language need only a new front end, and a new target CPU architecture need only a new back end - avoiding a combinatorial explosion of language-times-architecture compiler pairs", "Three-address code is a common concrete IR: each instruction has at most three operands, breaking every operation into the smallest possible individual steps"],
