  "adapter": {
    difficulty: "Intermediate", estimatedMinutes: 20,
    whatYoullLearn: ["The Adapter pattern - making incompatible interfaces work together", "A concrete scenario: integrating a third-party library whose interface doesn't match what your code expects", "Why Adapter wraps rather than modifies the incompatible class"],
    prerequisites: ["Strategy"],
    concept: "A travel power adapter doesn't change how your laptop charger works internally, and it doesn't change a foreign wall socket's own wiring either - it simply sits BETWEEN the two, translating one physical plug shape into the other, letting two genuinely incompatible things connect without either one needing to change. The Adapter pattern applies exactly this idea to INCOMPATIBLE INTERFACES: it wraps an existing class (often one you can't or shouldn't modify - like a third-party library) with a new class that translates calls into whatever shape your own code actually expects.\n\nA genuinely common real scenario: your application's code is written against a `PaymentProcessor` interface with a `pay(amount)` method, but a specific third-party payment library you need to integrate exposes a completely different, incompatible method: `library.executeTransaction(amountInCents, currency)`. Rather than rewriting your entire application to match that library's specific interface (invasive, and would need to be redone for every different library you might integrate), you write an ADAPTER class implementing YOUR `PaymentProcessor` interface, which internally translates each call into the library's actual expected format: `pay(amount) { library.executeTransaction(amount * 100, \"USD\"); }`.\n\nThe genuinely important discipline here: the adapter WRAPS the incompatible class, translating calls at the boundary - it never MODIFIES the original third-party library's own code at all (which you often couldn't modify even if you wanted to, and definitely shouldn't for a shared, externally-maintained dependency). This keeps your own application code clean, written entirely against YOUR OWN consistent interface, with all the messy, library-specific translation logic isolated in exactly one place - the adapter itself.",
    keyPoints: ["Adapter wraps an existing, incompatible class with a new class that translates calls into whatever interface your own code actually expects", "A common real scenario: integrating a third-party library whose method signatures don't match your application's own expected interface", "The adapter WRAPS and translates at the boundary - it never modifies the original incompatible class's own code, which is often external/shared and shouldn't be touched anyway"],
    commonMistakes: ["Trying to modify a third-party library's own source code to match your application's interface, instead of writing a wrapping Adapter class that translates at the boundary", "Scattering library-specific translation logic throughout your application code, instead of isolating it entirely inside one dedicated Adapter class"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to give a concrete third-party-library-integration example (like the payment processor scenario) - this is the most commonly asked, most immediately relatable real-world framing of Adapter"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Integrating any third-party API, SDK, or library whose interface doesn't naturally match your application's own internal conventions is a routine, constant real-world use of the Adapter pattern"],
    codeExample: { language: "java", code: "interface PaymentProcessor { void pay(double amount); }\n\nclass ThirdPartyLibrary { // can't/shouldn't modify this\n    void executeTransaction(int amountInCents, String currency) {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing \" + amountInCents + \" \" + currency);\n    }\n}\n\nclass PaymentAdapter implements PaymentProcessor {\n    private ThirdPartyLibrary library = new ThirdPartyLibrary();\n    public void pay(double amount) {\n        library.executeTransaction((int)(amount * 100), \"USD\"); // translates the call\n    }\n}\n\nPaymentProcessor processor = new PaymentAdapter();\nprocessor.pay(19.99); // your code only ever talks to YOUR interface" },
    mcqs: [
      { question: "What does the Adapter pattern do with an existing incompatible class?", options: ["Modifies the class's own internal source code directly", "Wraps it with a new class that translates calls into whatever interface your own code expects, without modifying the original class", "Deletes the incompatible class entirely", "Merges it with your application's own class"], correctIndex: 1 },
      { question: "What is a common, realistic scenario for using Adapter?", options: ["Creating a single instance of a class", "Integrating a third-party library whose method signatures don't match your application's own expected interface", "Selecting between multiple sorting algorithms", "Preventing a class from being extended"], correctIndex: 1 },
    ],
    assignment: "Design an Adapter that lets a LegacyLogger class (with a method like writeLog(String msg, int severity)) be used wherever your application expects a modern Logger interface (with a method like log(String message)).",
    goingDeeper: "Adapter comes in two structural flavors: OBJECT adapters (the version covered here, which HOLDS a reference to the wrapped object and delegates to it - the more common, flexible approach in languages without multiple inheritance) and CLASS adapters (which use multiple inheritance to inherit from both the target interface AND the adaptee simultaneously - only available in languages that actually support multiple inheritance of implementation, making the object-adapter approach the more broadly applicable, portable default in most modern OOP languages.",
    xpReward: 25, coinReward: 10,
  },
