    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "Before cloud computing, running a website meant BUYING actual physical servers, housing them somewhere with power and cooling, and hoping you'd guessed right about how much capacity you'd need - too little, and your site crashes under real traffic; too much, and you've paid for idle hardware sitting mostly unused. Cloud computing means renting computing resources (servers, storage, databases) from a provider (AWS, Azure, GCP) over the internet, ON DEMAND, paying only for what you actually use - genuinely closer to how you pay an electricity bill for what you consume, rather than buying and maintaining your own private power plant.\n\nCloud services are commonly categorized into three levels, each handing you a different amount of RAW CONTROL versus MANAGED CONVENIENCE: IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service - you rent raw virtual machines/storage/networking, and you're responsible for the operating system, runtime, and everything above it yourself - like renting an empty apartment and furnishing it completely yourself); PaaS (Platform as a Service - the provider also manages the OS and runtime, and you just deploy your actual application code - like a furnished apartment, ready to move into); SaaS (Software as a Service - you just use a complete, finished application over the internet, with zero infrastructure concerns at all - like staying in a hotel, where literally everything is handled for you).\n\nThe genuine, defining advantage cloud computing provides over owning physical hardware is ELASTICITY: automatically scaling resources UP during a traffic spike (a flash sale, a viral post) and back DOWN once it passes, paying only for the capacity actually consumed at each moment - this is precisely the capability that owning fixed physical hardware fundamentally cannot provide, no matter how well you plan your capacity in advance.",
    keyPoints: ["Cloud computing means renting computing resources on-demand from a provider (AWS/Azure/GCP), paying only for what you actually use, instead of buying and maintaining physical hardware", "IaaS (raw infrastructure, you manage everything above it), PaaS (provider manages the OS/runtime, you deploy code), SaaS (a complete, finished application, zero infrastructure concerns) - a spectrum of control versus convenience", "Elasticity - automatically scaling resources up during demand spikes and back down afterward, paying only for what's actually consumed - is cloud computing's genuine, defining advantage over fixed physical hardware"],
