    prerequisites: ["GCP Basics"],
    concept: "VIRTUALIZATION is the technology that makes cloud computing itself possible at its foundation: running MULTIPLE independent, isolated 'virtual computers' (each with their own operating system, believing they have exclusive access to dedicated hardware) all on top of ONE actual physical machine - genuinely similar to how an apartment building lets many separate households each have their own private, self-contained living space, all within one shared physical structure.\n\nA HYPERVISOR is the software layer that makes this possible - it sits between the actual physical hardware and each virtual machine, allocating and isolating CPU time, memory, and storage to each VM, while making each VM's own operating system believe it has direct, exclusive access to real hardware, when it's actually sharing that hardware carefully with several other VMs running alongside it. Type 1 hypervisors run directly on the physical hardware itself (used in real data centers/cloud providers, for maximum performance and isolation); Type 2 hypervisors run as a regular application on top of an existing host operating system (like VirtualBox running on your own personal laptop, genuinely convenient for local development/testing, but with somewhat more overhead than a Type 1 hypervisor).\n\nThis is precisely the foundational technology behind cloud providers renting you 'a virtual machine' at all (AWS EC2, Azure VMs, GCP Compute Engine, all covered in the previous three lessons) - one massive physical server in a data center is actually running dozens or hundreds of separate customers' VMs simultaneously, each one genuinely, securely isolated from the others, with the hypervisor being the actual mechanism making that isolation real and enforced, not merely a polite convention.",
