    goingDeeper: "Namespaces and cgroups are the actual Linux kernel features that make container isolation possible in practice: namespaces isolate what a process can SEE (its own filesystem view, its own process list, its own network interfaces, even though it's sharing the same underlying kernel as everything else), while cgroups (control groups) limit how much of the machine's actual CPU/memory/disk resources a given process (or container) is allowed to consume - Docker (the next lesson) is essentially a well-packaged, developer-friendly interface built on top of these lower-level kernel primitives, not magic of its own invention.",
