    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "Every single time a browser loads a webpage, or one backend service calls another, HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the actual language they're speaking to communicate - directly building on the transport-layer protocols (TCP, covered in this platform's own Computer Networks subject) that HTTP itself runs on top of. HTTP works as a REQUEST/RESPONSE exchange: a CLIENT (a browser, a mobile app, another server) sends a REQUEST to a SERVER, and the server sends back a RESPONSE - this is genuinely the entire shape of the protocol, repeated billions of times across the internet every single second.\n\nAn HTTP request has a METHOD (the ACTION being requested - covered in detail next), a URL (WHAT resource is being acted on), HEADERS (metadata about the request, like what content type the client accepts), and an optional BODY (actual data being sent, like a form submission's contents). A response mirrors this shape: a STATUS CODE (covered in its own dedicated lesson), headers, and an optional body (the actual requested content, or an error message).\n\nThe core HTTP METHODS each signal a distinct INTENT: GET requests a resource, without any side effects (fetching a webpage, viewing a profile); POST creates a new resource or submits data (creating a new user account); PUT replaces an entire existing resource; PATCH partially updates an existing resource (changing just one field); DELETE removes a resource - this exact GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE vocabulary is precisely what the next lesson's CRUD (Create/Read/Update/Delete) mapping is built directly on top of.",
    keyPoints: ["HTTP is a request/response protocol - a client sends a request, a server sends back a response, the entire foundational shape of web communication", "A request has a method, URL, headers, and an optional body; a response has a status code, headers, and an optional body", "Core HTTP methods signal distinct intent: GET (fetch, no side effects), POST (create/submit), PUT (replace entirely), PATCH (partially update), DELETE (remove)"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using GET for an action that has side effects (like deleting something) - GET is specifically meant to be safe and side-effect-free, and browsers/caches/crawlers may re-fetch a GET URL without warning", "Confusing PUT (replaces the ENTIRE resource) with PATCH (partially updates just specific fields) - genuinely different semantics, not interchangeable synonyms"],
