    concept: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight, TEXT-BASED data format used to represent structured data - genuinely the dominant format for API request/response bodies across the modern web, despite originating from JavaScript's own object-literal syntax (covered in this platform's own JavaScript path). JSON supports a small, genuinely simple set of data types: strings (`\"hello\"`), numbers (`42`, `3.14`), booleans (`true`/`false`), `null`, arrays (`[1, 2, 3]`), and objects (`{ \"key\": \"value\" }`) - objects and arrays can nest inside each other arbitrarily deep, letting JSON represent genuinely complex structured data.\n\n`{ \"name\": \"Priya\", \"age\": 20, \"skills\": [\"Java\", \"Python\"], \"address\": { \"city\": \"Hyderabad\" } }` demonstrates this nesting directly: a string, a number, an array of strings, and a NESTED object all inside one JSON object - genuinely this exact structural flexibility, combined with being simple enough to read and write by hand, is a large part of why JSON became so pervasive.\n\nJSON largely DISPLACED XML (eXtensible Markup Language, an older, more verbose data format) as the dominant API format specifically because JSON is more COMPACT (no matching open/close tags needed for every single value) and maps directly onto the native data structures (objects, arrays) that virtually every modern programming language already has built in - parsing JSON into a usable in-memory object is typically a single built-in function call in most languages (`JSON.parse()` in JavaScript, `json.loads()` in Python), whereas XML historically required more involved parsing libraries and a more verbose in-memory representation to work with.",
