    concept: "CRUD stands for Create, Read, Update, Delete - the four fundamental operations virtually EVERY application needs to perform on its data, regardless of what that data actually represents (users, products, blog posts, orders) - genuinely a near-universal pattern once you recognize it, directly connecting to and building on the HTTP methods covered in this subject's first lesson.\n\nThe mapping onto REST is direct and genuinely worth memorizing as a single, unified table: CREATE maps to `POST /resources` (create a new resource); READ maps to `GET /resources` (list all) or `GET /resources/{id}` (fetch one specific resource); UPDATE maps to `PUT /resources/{id}` (replace entirely) or `PATCH /resources/{id}` (partially update); DELETE maps to `DELETE /resources/{id}`.\n\nA concrete, complete example for a `Book` resource: `POST /books` (create a new book, with the new book's data in the request body) returns the newly-created book (typically with its assigned ID); `GET /books` (list all books) or `GET /books/42` (fetch book 42 specifically); `PUT /books/42` (replace book 42's ENTIRE data) or `PATCH /books/42` (update just, say, book 42's price); `DELETE /books/42` (remove book 42 entirely) - this exact CRUD-to-REST mapping is genuinely THE most common, most foundational pattern underlying real-world API design, and internalizing it deeply means you can correctly design (or confidently critique) the majority of typical REST endpoints for almost ANY resource type, having genuinely never seen that specific resource before.",
