    goingDeeper: "Even 'stateless' token-based authentication has a genuine practical wrinkle worth knowing: REVOKING a token before its natural expiration (like immediately after a user changes their password, or an account is suspended) is genuinely harder than with sessions, since the server has no central record of currently-valid tokens to simply delete - real systems handle this with short-lived tokens plus a separate refresh mechanism, or a deliberately-maintained (and yes, genuinely somewhat stateful) token-blocklist for the rare cases immediate revocation is truly needed, a real, honest trade-off worth acknowledging rather than treating token-based auth as having zero downsides.",
    xpReward: 30, coinReward: 12,
