    assignment: "Explain, in your own words, why encryption in transit (HTTPS) and encryption at rest (an encrypted database) are both genuinely necessary, describing a specific attack scenario each one specifically protects against that the other doesn't.",
    goingDeeper: "Asymmetric encryption's public/private key pairs are also the exact foundation of DIGITAL SIGNATURES (used differently from encrypting data for confidentiality): signing data with your PRIVATE key lets ANYONE verify, using your corresponding PUBLIC key, that the data genuinely came from you and hasn't been tampered with - this is precisely the same underlying public/private key mechanism from this lesson, just used to prove AUTHENTICITY and INTEGRITY rather than to keep data secret, directly connecting to how JWT signatures (this platform's REST APIs subject) can alternatively use asymmetric keys instead of a shared secret.",
