    goingDeeper: "Load balancers themselves can become a SINGLE POINT OF FAILURE if there's only one - if the load balancer itself crashes, ALL traffic to every backend server stops, regardless of how healthy those backend servers still are. Real production systems address this by running MULTIPLE load balancers (often with DNS or a lower-level networking mechanism distributing traffic across them), ensuring no single component anywhere in the architecture represents an unrecoverable failure point.",
