    whatYoullLearn: ["Converting between binary, decimal, octal, and hexadecimal systematically", "Why octal and hexadecimal exist specifically as binary shorthand", "Representing negative numbers with two's complement"],
    prerequisites: ["Binary"],
    concept: "Building directly on the Binary lesson, several NUMBER SYSTEMS are genuinely relevant in computing, each a different BASE (the number of distinct digit symbols before 'carrying over' to the next position): binary (base-2), octal (base-8, digits 0-7), decimal (base-10, digits 0-9, what humans use daily), and hexadecimal (base-16, digits 0-9 plus A-F for 10-15).\n\nOctal and hexadecimal exist specifically as CONVENIENT SHORTHANDS for binary, not as independently 'better' number systems in their own right: exactly 3 binary bits map to one octal digit (since 2^3=8), and exactly 4 binary bits map to one hexadecimal digit (since 2^4=16) - this clean mapping is precisely why hex/octal are used constantly in computing contexts (memory addresses, color codes) specifically as a genuinely more compact, more human-readable way to write what's fundamentally still binary data underneath.\n\nRepresenting NEGATIVE numbers in binary requires a specific convention, since there's no natural '-' symbol available - TWO'S COMPLEMENT is the standard approach virtually all modern computers use: to negate a binary number, INVERT every bit (flip every 0 to 1 and every 1 to 0), then ADD 1 to the result. This specific scheme has a genuinely elegant property worth appreciating: it lets a CPU perform SUBTRACTION using the exact same ADDITION circuitry it already has for regular addition (subtracting B from A is really just computing A plus the two's-complement negation of B) - a real, deliberate hardware-simplification benefit, not an arbitrary mathematical curiosity.",
    keyPoints: ["Binary (base-2), octal (base-8), decimal (base-10), and hexadecimal (base-16) are the number systems relevant in computing - each a different base with its own set of digit symbols", "Octal (3 bits per digit) and hexadecimal (4 bits per digit) exist specifically as compact, convenient shorthands for binary, mapping cleanly onto groups of bits", "Two's complement represents negative binary numbers by inverting all bits and adding 1 - this lets a CPU reuse the same addition circuitry for subtraction, a genuine hardware-simplification benefit"],
