    prerequisites: ["Cache"],
    concept: "An INTERRUPT is a signal that temporarily PAUSES the CPU's current, ongoing instruction execution to immediately handle some other, genuinely urgent event - a keyboard key being pressed, a network packet arriving, a timer expiring - allowing the CPU to respond IMMEDIATELY when something important genuinely happens, rather than needing to constantly, repeatedly CHECK ('are you ready yet? are you ready yet?') whether some external device has anything new for it.\n\nThis 'constantly checking' alternative is called POLLING, and interrupts are dramatically more EFFICIENT specifically because they eliminate polling's genuine, real waste: without interrupts, the CPU would need to repeatedly, continuously check every single possible input device in a tight loop, burning genuine CPU cycles on checks that come back 'nothing new' the overwhelming majority of the time - interrupts instead let the CPU do genuinely useful work the REST of the time, only being explicitly notified the SPECIFIC MOMENT something actually, genuinely needs its attention.\n\nWhen an interrupt occurs, the CPU follows a specific, careful sequence: it SAVES its current state (the Program Counter and other relevant register values, from the Registers lesson, so it can correctly RESUME exactly where it left off afterward), jumps to a specific INTERRUPT HANDLER (a small, dedicated piece of code specifically written to deal with that particular type of interrupt), executes that handler completely, then RESTORES its previously-saved state and resumes the original, interrupted program exactly where it had left off - entirely transparently, as if the interruption had genuinely never happened at all from that original program's own perspective.",
