    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "Sending a letter through a postal system involves several genuinely distinct, independent concerns: what you actually WRITE (the message itself), how it's PACKAGED (an envelope with an address), how it physically TRAVELS (trucks, planes, sorting facilities) - each concern handled by a different part of the system, largely oblivious to the OTHER layers' own internal details. The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model applies exactly this same layered-separation idea to computer networking, defining SEVEN distinct layers, each with its OWN specific responsibility, each layer only needing to know how to talk to the layers immediately above and below it.\n\nFrom bottom (closest to actual physical hardware) to top (closest to the actual application/user): Layer 1 PHYSICAL (the actual electrical signals, light pulses, or radio waves genuinely carrying raw bits); Layer 2 DATA LINK (organizing raw bits into FRAMES, and handling communication between devices on the SAME local network - MAC addresses live here); Layer 3 NETWORK (routing data ACROSS different networks, using IP addresses - covered in this subject's own dedicated IP Addressing and Routing lessons); Layer 4 TRANSPORT (ensuring reliable, ordered delivery - TCP - or a simpler, faster delivery without those guarantees - UDP - both covered in their own dedicated lessons); Layer 5 SESSION (managing an ongoing conversation between two devices); Layer 6 PRESENTATION (data format translation, encryption/compression); Layer 7 APPLICATION (the actual protocols application software directly uses - HTTP, DNS, SMTP, all covered in their own dedicated lessons in this subject).\n\nA genuinely important, honest nuance worth knowing: real-world networking doesn't implement these exact seven distinct layers literally in practice - the actual TCP/IP model (covered in the very next lesson) is what real systems genuinely use, with only four layers, several of which loosely CORRESPOND to multiple OSI layers combined together. The OSI model remains genuinely useful anyway specifically as a shared, universal TEACHING and troubleshooting VOCABULARY - saying 'this is a Layer 3 routing issue' versus 'this is a Layer 7 application issue' precisely communicates roughly WHERE in a genuinely complex networking stack a specific problem likely lives, even though no real system literally implements all seven layers as separate, distinct pieces of actual code.",
