    prerequisites: ["OSI Model"],
    concept: "While OSI (previous lesson) is primarily a conceptual TEACHING model, TCP/IP is the ACTUAL, practical model the real internet and virtually every real computer network genuinely runs on - named directly after its two most foundational protocols (TCP and IP, both covered in their own dedicated lessons in this subject). TCP/IP organizes networking into just FOUR layers, each loosely CORRESPONDING to one or more of OSI's seven: the NETWORK ACCESS layer (combining OSI's Physical and Data Link layers together); the INTERNET layer (corresponding to OSI's Network layer - IP addressing/routing lives here); the TRANSPORT layer (corresponding directly to OSI's own Transport layer - TCP/UDP); and the APPLICATION layer (combining OSI's Session, Presentation, AND Application layers together into one single layer - HTTP, DNS, SMTP all live here).\n\nTCP/IP became the genuine, universal internet standard specifically because of a deliberate, foundational design decision: it was built to be ROBUST and DECENTRALIZED from the very start (a direct legacy of its origins in ARPANET, a US Department of Defense-funded research network explicitly designed to keep functioning correctly even if parts of the network were destroyed or disconnected) - no single, central point of control or failure was ever baked into its core design, a genuinely deliberate architectural choice that turned out to scale remarkably well as the internet itself grew from a small research network into the truly global, planet-spanning network it is today.\n\nThis subject's remaining lessons (IP Addressing, Routing, TCP, UDP, and the Application-layer protocols like HTTP/DNS/SMTP) map directly onto TCP/IP's own four layers, giving you the genuine, practical, real-world model actual networking is built around, complementing the OSI model's more conceptual, teaching-oriented seven-layer framing from the previous lesson.",
