    prerequisites: ["TCP/IP"],
    concept: "An IP (Internet Protocol) address is a unique numeric identifier assigned to every single device connected to a network, genuinely functioning as that device's own specific 'postal address' - letting data know precisely WHERE to actually go, directly connecting to the OSI/TCP/IP models' own Network/Internet layer covered in the previous two lessons.\n\nIPv4 (the ORIGINAL, still-widely-used addressing scheme) uses a 32-bit address, conventionally written as four decimal numbers separated by dots (`192.168.1.1`), each number ranging 0-255 - genuinely providing about 4.3 BILLION possible unique addresses total, which sounded practically limitless back when IPv4 was originally designed in the 1980s, but has since genuinely, actually run out, given the internet's explosive real-world growth (billions of phones, computers, and IoT devices all needing their own address). IPv6 is the newer addressing scheme specifically designed to solve this exhaustion problem, using a dramatically larger 128-bit address (`2001:0db8:85a3::8a2e:0370:7334`) - providing a number of possible unique addresses so astronomically large it's genuinely expected to remain sufficient for the truly foreseeable future.\n\nA genuinely important, practical distinction: PUBLIC IP addresses are globally unique and directly reachable from anywhere on the internet (your home router's own address, as seen from the outside internet); PRIVATE IP addresses (like the common `192.168.x.x` or `10.x.x.x` ranges) are only meaningful WITHIN one specific local network, and are deliberately reused identically across millions of different private home/office networks worldwide, since they're never directly reachable from the internet at large - this exact private-addressing scheme, combined with NAT (Network Address Translation, letting many devices on one private network all share a SINGLE public IP address when actually communicating out to the internet), is precisely one of the practical techniques that helped genuinely stretch IPv4's own limited address space considerably further than its raw 4.3 billion addresses would otherwise have allowed on their own.",
