    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "Every other subject in this course teaches you HOW to build something - a working algorithm, a database, a network. Theory of Computation asks a genuinely different, more fundamental question: what is ACTUALLY, MATHEMATICALLY possible to compute at all, regardless of how clever your code is or how fast your hardware gets? It studies computation itself as a mathematical object, abstracting completely away from any specific programming language or physical machine.\n\nThe subject builds around three genuinely foundational questions: WHAT can be computed at all (some problems are mathematically, provably impossible to solve by ANY algorithm, ever - covered in the Decidability lesson)? HOW EFFICIENTLY can something be computed, and are there problems where even a genuinely correct algorithm would take longer than the lifetime of the universe to finish (covered in the Complexity Classes lesson)? And WHAT KIND of abstract machine is actually needed to solve a given class of problem - simpler machines (finite automata) can only recognize simpler patterns, while more powerful machines (Turing machines) can compute anything genuinely computable at all.\n\nThis might sound purely academic, but it directly explains real, everyday tools: every regular expression engine you've ever used is literally implementing a finite automaton (covered in the very next lesson); every compiler's parser relies on the pushdown-automata/context-free-grammar theory covered later in this subject; and knowing that a problem is provably UNDECIDABLE (like the Halting Problem) tells you, with mathematical certainty, to stop searching for a general algorithm that simply cannot exist, rather than wasting real engineering time chasing an impossible solution.",
