    prerequisites: ["Introduction"],
    concept: "A FINITE AUTOMATON (FA) is the simplest genuine model of computation covered in this subject - a machine with a FIXED, finite number of STATES, reading an input string one symbol at a time, moving between states according to TRANSITION rules, and either ACCEPTING or REJECTING the string once every symbol has been read, based on whether it ends in a designated 'accepting' state - genuinely similar to a simple turnstile or vending machine's own internal state logic, just formalized mathematically.\n\nA DETERMINISTIC Finite Automaton (DFA) has EXACTLY one possible transition for every state-symbol combination - given the current state and the next input symbol, there's never any ambiguity about which state comes next. A NON-DETERMINISTIC Finite Automaton (NFA) relaxes this: from a given state, a symbol might have MULTIPLE possible next states (or even zero), and an NFA accepts a string if AT LEAST ONE possible sequence of choices leads to acceptance - genuinely like exploring several possible paths simultaneously rather than committing to just one.\n\nA genuinely remarkable, foundational result worth knowing precisely: DFAs and NFAs are PROVABLY EQUIVALENT in computational power - every NFA can be mechanically converted into an equivalent DFA (via the 'subset construction' algorithm, where each DFA state represents a SET of possible NFA states) that accepts exactly the same language, even though the resulting DFA might have exponentially more states. This means NFAs offer no genuinely NEW computational capability over DFAs - they're purely a CONVENIENCE, often letting you design a given pattern-recognizer far more simply and intuitively than an equivalent DFA would allow, while a computer can still always mechanically convert it to a DFA for actual efficient execution.",
    keyPoints: ["A finite automaton has a fixed number of states, reads input one symbol at a time via transition rules, and accepts/rejects based on its final state - the simplest genuine model of computation", "A DFA has exactly one transition per state-symbol pair (no ambiguity); an NFA can have multiple (or zero) possible transitions, accepting if ANY possible path leads to acceptance", "DFAs and NFAs are provably EQUIVALENT in power - every NFA can be mechanically converted to an equivalent DFA (subset construction) - NFAs are purely a design convenience, not a genuine capability increase"],
