    assignment: "Explain, in your own words, why a Turing machine's stack-free, freely-navigable infinite tape gives it strictly more power than a pushdown automaton's stack, even though both are technically 'unbounded' in size.",
    goingDeeper: "A genuinely important, related concept: a 'Universal Turing Machine' is a single, specific Turing machine that can simulate ANY other Turing machine, given a description of that other machine (encoded on its own tape) plus its input - this is precisely the theoretical foundation behind the modern STORED-PROGRAM computer architecture (where a single physical machine can run any program, rather than needing separate hardware built for each specific task), directly connecting this subject's own theoretical Turing machine concept to the very real, practical Computer Organization subject's coverage of how actual CPUs work.",
