    assignment: "Explain, in your own words, why the self-referential program in Turing's diagonalization proof creates a genuine logical contradiction no matter what the assumed HALTS function predicts.",
    goingDeeper: "REDUCTION is the standard technique for proving OTHER problems are also undecidable, beyond just the Halting Problem itself: if you can show that solving some new problem X would ALSO let you solve the Halting Problem (by cleverly using a hypothetical solution to X as a building block), then X must ALSO be undecidable, since we already know the Halting Problem itself has no solution - this exact reduction technique is how a genuinely large family of other real problems (like determining whether two arbitrary programs are functionally equivalent) have all been proven undecidable too, without needing a separate, from-scratch diagonalization proof for each one individually.",
    xpReward: 40, coinReward: 18,
