    goingDeeper: "A ripple-carry adder's genuine downside is SPEED - the final, highest bit position's sum isn't correct until the carry has genuinely 'rippled' sequentially through every single lower bit position first, creating real propagation delay that grows with the number of bits. A CARRY-LOOKAHEAD adder is a more sophisticated, faster design that computes each bit position's carry more directly (using additional logic to predict carries ahead of time, rather than waiting for them to ripple through sequentially) - a genuine, practical example of the general electrical-engineering trade-off between circuit simplicity and circuit speed.",
