    prerequisites: ["Introduction to NLP"],
    concept: "Raw text (a sentence, a document) is fundamentally just a long STRING - no ML model (from this platform's own Machine Learning subject) can operate on a raw string directly, since every model expects numeric input. TEXT PREPROCESSING is the essential first step converting messy raw text into a clean, structured form suitable for the next step (turning it into numbers, covered in the next lesson).\n\nTOKENIZATION is the foundational preprocessing step: splitting a text string into individual units called TOKENS - most commonly WORDS ('the cat sat' becomes ['the', 'cat', 'sat']), though modern systems (including the transformers covered later in this subject) frequently use SUB-WORD tokens instead (splitting rare/complex words into smaller meaningful pieces, like 'unhappiness' into ['un', 'happi', 'ness']) specifically to handle words the system has genuinely never seen before during training, which pure whole-word tokenization cannot handle gracefully at all.\n\nSeveral further common preprocessing steps, each genuinely worth knowing precisely: LOWERCASING (treating 'Cat' and 'cat' as the same token, avoiding spurious duplicate vocabulary entries); STOP-WORD REMOVAL (dropping extremely common, low-information words like 'the', 'is', 'a' that rarely help distinguish one document's actual meaning from another's); STEMMING (crudely chopping words down to a common root by simple, fast pattern-based rules - 'running'/'runs'/'ran' might all become 'run', though stemming can sometimes produce a genuinely non-real word); and LEMMATIZATION (a more linguistically-informed version of the same idea, using actual dictionary/grammar knowledge to reduce a word to its genuinely valid base 'lemma' form - slower than stemming, but produces genuinely correct base words instead of stemming's occasionally-crude approximations).",
    keyPoints: ["Raw text must be preprocessed into a clean, structured form before any ML model can use it, since models require numeric input, not raw strings", "Tokenization splits text into individual tokens (words, or sub-words for handling never-before-seen words) - the foundational preprocessing step", "Further common steps: lowercasing, stop-word removal (dropping low-information common words), stemming (fast, crude root-chopping), and lemmatization (slower, linguistically-correct base-word reduction)"],
    commonMistakes: ["Skipping tokenization/preprocessing and assuming a model can work directly on raw, unprocessed text strings", "Confusing stemming with lemmatization - stemming is a fast, crude, rule-based approximation that can produce non-real words, while lemmatization uses genuine linguistic knowledge to produce a correct base form"],
