    prerequisites: ["Introduction to Parallel Computing"],
    concept: "FLYNN'S TAXONOMY is the classic, foundational framework for classifying computer architectures based on two independent dimensions: how many INSTRUCTION streams a system processes, and how many DATA streams it processes simultaneously - crossing these two dimensions (single or multiple, for each) produces exactly four categories, each genuinely worth knowing precisely, since real hardware maps cleanly onto each.\n\nSISD (Single Instruction, Single Data) is the classic traditional, sequential single-core processor: one instruction stream operates on one data stream at a time - genuinely no parallelism at all, the traditional baseline this entire subject's remaining lessons are building beyond. SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) applies the exact SAME single instruction simultaneously across MULTIPLE different data elements at once - this is precisely the architecture underlying modern GPUs (covered in this subject's own GPU Computing lesson) and CPU vector/SIMD instruction extensions, genuinely well-suited to data-parallel workloads (applying the identical operation, like brightening a pixel, across millions of data elements simultaneously). MISD (Multiple Instruction, Single Data) applies MULTIPLE different instructions to the SAME single data stream simultaneously - a genuinely rare, niche category in practice, mostly seen in specialized fault-tolerant systems (running the same data through several different, independently-verified computation paths simultaneously as a safety/verification check, rather than for raw performance). MIMD (Multiple Instruction, Multiple Data) runs MULTIPLE independent instruction streams, each operating on its OWN independent data stream - this is precisely the architecture underlying modern multi-core CPUs and distributed computing clusters, where each core/machine genuinely runs its own separate program on its own separate data.\n\nThis taxonomy genuinely matters practically, worth internalizing clearly: it directly explains WHY different hardware suits different workloads - a data-parallel task (apply the same filter to every pixel in an image) maps naturally and efficiently onto SIMD hardware (a GPU), while a task-parallel workload (run a web server handling many completely independent user requests simultaneously) maps naturally onto MIMD hardware (a multi-core CPU) - choosing hardware/architecture that MATCHES your actual workload's genuine parallelism pattern is precisely the practical decision this taxonomy directly informs.",
