  "synchronization-race-conditions": {
    difficulty: "Advanced", estimatedMinutes: 25,
    whatYoullLearn: ["Race conditions as the fundamental correctness hazard of shared-memory parallelism", "Locks/mutexes as the standard synchronization mechanism preventing them", "Deadlock as a genuine risk introduced by using locks incorrectly"],
    prerequisites: ["Parallel Programming Models"],
    concept: "The previous lesson's threads (sharing direct access to the SAME memory) introduce a genuinely serious correctness hazard worth understanding precisely: a RACE CONDITION occurs when multiple threads access and modify the SAME shared data simultaneously, and the final, resulting outcome genuinely depends on the unpredictable TIMING/ORDER of exactly how their individual operations happen to interleave - directly connecting to and extending this platform's own Operating Systems subject's coverage of this exact same hazard from the process/thread-scheduling angle.\n\nThe classic, concrete illustrating example: two threads both execute `balance = balance + 10` on the SAME shared bank balance variable simultaneously - this single line of code is NOT genuinely atomic (indivisible) at the hardware level; it actually involves multiple separate steps (READ the current balance, ADD 10, WRITE the new value back) - if both threads happen to READ the SAME initial balance BEFORE either one has finished WRITING its own updated result back, one thread's entire update gets silently, invisibly LOST (overwritten by the other), resulting in the balance only increasing by 10 total instead of the genuinely correct 20 - a real, serious correctness bug that occurs unpredictably, depending purely on the exact timing of thread execution, making these bugs genuinely difficult to reliably reproduce and debug.\n\nA LOCK (or MUTEX - 'mutual exclusion') is the standard synchronization mechanism directly preventing race conditions: a thread must successfully ACQUIRE a lock before entering a CRITICAL SECTION (the code that reads/modifies shared data), and must RELEASE that lock afterward - if another thread attempts to acquire the SAME lock while it's already held, that second thread must genuinely WAIT until the first thread releases it, guaranteeing only ONE thread can ever be inside the critical section at any given time, directly preventing the race condition entirely by design.\n\nLocks, however, introduce their own genuine, distinct risk worth knowing precisely: DEADLOCK occurs when two (or more) threads each hold a lock the OTHER thread genuinely needs, and each is simultaneously WAITING for the other to release its own lock first - neither can ever proceed, and the program genuinely freezes permanently (directly connecting to and extending the exact same deadlock concept already covered in depth in this platform's own Operating Systems subject, just now viewed specifically through this subject's parallel-computing lens) - correctly avoiding deadlock (via a consistent lock-acquisition ORDER across all threads, among other established techniques) is a genuinely essential, real skill for writing correct multi-threaded parallel code.",
    keyPoints: ["A race condition occurs when multiple threads access/modify the same shared data simultaneously and the final result depends unpredictably on the exact timing of their interleaved execution - a genuine, serious correctness hazard", "Locks/mutexes prevent race conditions by ensuring only one thread can be inside a critical section (code touching shared data) at any given time, forcing other threads to wait", "Locks introduce their own genuine risk: deadlock, where two or more threads each hold a lock the other needs and wait on each other forever, permanently freezing the program - avoided via disciplined, consistent lock-acquisition ordering"],
    commonMistakes: ["Assuming a single line of code like 'balance = balance + 10' executes as one indivisible, atomic operation at the hardware level, missing that it actually involves separate read/add/write steps that can interleave incorrectly between threads", "Adding locks to prevent race conditions without a consistent, disciplined acquisition order across all threads, genuinely risking introducing deadlock as an unintended side effect of the fix itself"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to walk through the classic shared-bank-balance race condition example step by step (read, add, write interleaving incorrectly), and to explain precisely how a lock/mutex prevents it, plus how deadlock can arise from locks used incorrectly"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Race conditions are a genuinely common, real source of hard-to-reproduce bugs in real multi-threaded production software (banking systems, web servers handling concurrent requests) - correct lock usage is essential, everyday practical knowledge for anyone writing multi-threaded code"],
    codeExample: { language: "text", code: "Race condition (balance starts at 100, both threads add 10):\n  Thread A: read balance (100)\n  Thread B: read balance (100)   <- both read BEFORE either writes\n  Thread A: write 100+10 = 110\n  Thread B: write 100+10 = 110   <- B's write overwrites A's update!\n  Result: balance = 110 (WRONG - should be 120)\n\nWith a lock: Thread A acquires lock -> read/add/write -> release lock\n             Thread B must WAIT until A releases, then does the same\n  Result: balance = 120 (correct)" },
    mcqs: [
      { question: "Why can 'balance = balance + 10' produce an incorrect result when run simultaneously by two threads?", options: ["This line of code is always perfectly safe with no possible issue", "It actually involves separate read, add, and write steps that aren't atomic - if both threads read the same value before either writes back, one thread's update gets silently lost", "Addition operations are inherently impossible to parallelize", "The balance variable cannot be shared between threads at all"], correctIndex: 1 },
      { question: "What is deadlock, and how does it typically arise?", options: ["Deadlock is the same thing as a race condition", "Two or more threads each hold a lock the other needs and wait on each other forever, often arising from inconsistent lock-acquisition order across threads", "Deadlock only occurs in single-threaded programs", "Deadlock is a performance optimization technique, not a bug"], correctIndex: 1 },
    ],
    assignment: "Walk through, step by step, how the classic shared-bank-balance race condition example produces an incorrect result of 110 instead of 120, then explain precisely how introducing a lock around the critical section fixes it.",
    goingDeeper: "LOCK-FREE / ATOMIC operations (using special hardware-supported instructions like 'compare-and-swap') offer a genuinely different alternative to locks entirely for certain simple operations: rather than making a thread WAIT for a lock, an atomic operation guarantees the ENTIRE read-modify-write sequence happens as one indivisible hardware step with no possible interleaving at all - genuinely useful specifically for simple operations (incrementing a counter) where the overhead of acquiring/releasing a full lock would be disproportionately expensive relative to the trivial work actually being protected.",
    xpReward: 35, coinReward: 15,
  },
