    prerequisites: ["Introduction to Microprocessors"],
    concept: "A microprocessor's internal ARCHITECTURE consists of several core components working together, each with a genuinely concrete, specific physical role: the ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit) performs the actual arithmetic (addition, subtraction) and logical (AND, OR, comparison) operations an instruction requires; REGISTERS are small, extremely fast storage locations directly built into the chip itself (holding data currently being actively worked on - genuinely much faster to access than external main memory, directly connecting to the Computer Organization subject's own memory-hierarchy speed/cost trade-off coverage); and the CONTROL UNIT orchestrates everything else, reading each instruction and generating the precise electrical control signals that direct the ALU/registers/memory to actually carry out that instruction's specific operation.\n\nThese concrete physical components are precisely what PHYSICALLY IMPLEMENTS the abstract fetch-decode-execute cycle already covered conceptually in the Computer Organization subject: FETCH retrieves the next instruction from memory into the chip (using a program counter register tracking exactly where execution currently is); DECODE has the control unit interpret exactly what operation this specific instruction's bit pattern represents; EXECUTE has the control unit generate the actual signals directing the ALU/registers to genuinely carry out that decoded operation - this subject's own concrete architecture lessons are precisely what makes that previously-abstract cycle concretely, physically real.\n\nThe classic 8085 (8-bit) and 8086 (16-bit) microprocessors, though genuinely old by modern standards, remain the standard TEACHING reference architecture across most academic microprocessor courses specifically because their genuinely SIMPLE internal structure (a small, manageable register set - accumulator, general-purpose registers - and a straightforward, small instruction set) makes the underlying concepts concretely graspable, without today's vastly more complex modern CPUs' many additional layers (multiple cores, deep pipelining, branch prediction) obscuring these exact same foundational ideas - genuinely worth learning on these simpler, historical chips first, precisely because the core concepts transfer directly to understanding any modern processor's far more complex internals.",
