    assignment: "Write a short assembly program (in the style of this lesson's code example) that loads two immediate values, subtracts one from the other, and stores the result in memory - explain each instruction's role.",
    goingDeeper: "ASSEMBLERS come in two genuine variants worth knowing: a ONE-PASS assembler processes the source code just once, requiring every symbol (label/variable) to be already DEFINED before it's first referenced; a TWO-PASS assembler processes the source TWICE - the first pass just collects every symbol's definition/address, and the second pass then correctly resolves ALL references (including ones referencing a label defined LATER in the code, a 'forward reference') - two-pass assemblers are more genuinely flexible for real, non-trivial programs specifically because real code frequently needs to jump forward to a label that hasn't been defined yet at that point in the source.",
